From df59e5ca03a4d23e657f950e04487bb3d3b99208 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 06:24:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] fix(#48): a mutant's id is the same under every
 interpreter, and an id recorded under the old rule still resolves

The defect, verbatim: `_content_mutant_id` hashes `ast.dump(mutated_node, include_attributes=True)`.
Python 3.13 changed `ast.dump`'s default output (empty optional fields are omitted), so the same
mutation of the same source has one id under 3.12 and another under 3.13+. A flag recorded under one
family does not resolve under the other, and nothing says why.

Why the obvious fix is wrong: `show_empty=True` on 3.13+ is not a rule, it is a flag that only exists
there, and it still reads whatever defaults the running version fills into a hand-built node (3.13 fills
omitted list fields with `[]`, 3.12 leaves them unset). The id has to stop reading the interpreter, so it
hashes a canonical serialization of our own (`Wesker.canonical_ast.canonical_ast`): a node's own
`_fields` then `_attributes` in declaration order, a field that is None, an empty list or unset is not
written whichever version defaulted it, and a constant is spelled by type name and `repr`.

What each state means: `resolve_recorded_mutant_id(recorded, current, legacy)` is the pure decision a
consumer applies to a stored id. `current`: already an id of this function's present universe.
`migrated`: exactly one current mutant had this id under the old rule, in either family, and the second
element is its new id. `ambiguous`: the old rule issued the id for more than one current mutant, so
neither is applied. `unmapped`: no current mutant of this function maps from it (the source or the
policy moved), so the caller names it. `mutant_id_migration(func_node)` regenerates the function's
mutants and joins each new id to the id the old rule issued under py312 and py313+ (the stdlib dump is
re-implemented per family in `legacy_ast_dump`, byte-equal to the running interpreter's own on every
function of engine.py under both 3.12 and 3.14). Both are exported from `Wesker`.

Measured on Wesker's own package (841 functions, all categories, exhaustive): 31211 mutants. The ids are
identical under 3.12.13 and 3.14.6 for all 31211 (order and content, every function). The ids the old
rule issued under 3.12 resolve through the 3.14 emulation, and the ids it issued under 3.14 resolve
through the 3.12 emulation: 31211 of 31211 each way map to the one current mutant, 0 ambiguous, 0
unmapped. Every mutant id moves once; the policy id moves with the observation text
(9.a5aeead0de4f -> 9.bd305bdfd9f8, two-sign 9+neg.6035bc2f613d -> 9+neg.f2bb2b1e5f8b); POLICY_VERSION
stays 9 because the universe did not move.

What was NOT closed: the flag store lives in Detective (`Detective/equivalents.py`, `.detective/`) and
nothing there calls the migration yet, so flags recorded before this commit still resolve only once that
consumer lands (it is written up for the integrator). A function's OUTPUT mutants depend on the run's
observed return types, so migrating them needs the same `observed_return_types` generation was given
(`mutant_id_migration` takes it). An id from a source or policy older than the one regenerated here is
`unmapped` by design.

Receipt: not pinned here (the new modules are serialization and a join, not a manufactured decision;
`resolve_recorded_mutant_id` is pinned by the integrator after Wave 4).
Tests: tests/test_canonical_ast_intent.py (14), test_mu_minus_families_intent.py, test_policy.py, the
four guard files and test_engine.py: 264 passed under 3.14.6 and under 3.12.13, run with
PYTHONUNBUFFERED unset. With the fix disarmed (the old hash restored) 3 of the id-bearing tests fail.
ruff clean under pinned 0.16.7.
